window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.8","title":"Effect Size and Practical Significance","goal":"Compute Cohen's d for the memory-training study by hand, then see a huge sample make a tiny effect significant while d stays tiny.","question":"A school tests a memory-training program with 25 trained students and 25 untrained students on the same memory test. The trained group averages 78 and the control group 70, and the t-test is significant. The principal asks a harder question: how BIG is the improvement in a way that does not depend on the sample size, and would a tiny improvement also look 'significant' in a very large study?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the two groups","text":"H0: the true average memory scores of trained and control students are equal. H1: they differ. The scores below are built to match the lesson: trained mean 78 (SD 10), control mean 70 (SD 12), 25 students per group.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ntrained = np.array([95.69, 56.78, 81.95, 73.61, 74.58, 76.59, 61.32, 76.45, 71.09, 106.54, 80.32, 75.43, 76.03, 72.76, 69.48, 75.11, 82.49, 76.39, 86.52, 76.72, 78.62, 91.5, 83.03, 74.14, 76.87])\ncontrol = np.array([62.92, 68.16, 88.37, 77.33, 52.05, 70.03, 63.23, 71.72, 52.41, 72.74, 72.67, 87.4, 73.57, 75.7, 53.68, 94.84, 49.03, 82.19, 66.46, 60.41, 62.87, 62.7, 74.26, 68.88, 86.38])\n\nfor name, g in [('trained', trained), ('control', control)]:\n    print(f'{name}: n = {len(g)}, mean = {g.mean():.2f}, sd = {g.std(ddof=1):.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for arrays and square roots, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools for the t-test.","Store the 25 scores of the trained students.","Store the 25 scores of the control students.","Loop over both groups with their names, so one piece of code describes each group.","Print each group's size, mean and sample SD (ddof=1). We always look at the numbers before testing them."],"meaning":"The trained group averages 78.00 (SD 10.00) and the control group 70.00 (SD 12.00), with 25 students each. The raw gap is 8 points, but 8 points means little until we know how spread out scores are. That is what Cohen's d measures.","out":"trained: n = 25, mean = 78.00, sd = 10.00\ncontrol: n = 25, mean = 70.00, sd = 12.00","figs":[]},{"title":"Cohen's d by hand","text":"Cohen's d is the gap between the means measured in pooled standard deviations. The pooled SD is a weighted average of the two group SDs, so d does not depend on the sample size.","code":"n1, n2 = len(trained), len(control)\ns1, s2 = trained.std(ddof=1), control.std(ddof=1)\n\nsp = np.sqrt(((n1 - 1) * s1**2 + (n2 - 1) * s2**2) / (n1 + n2 - 2))\nd = (trained.mean() - control.mean()) / sp\ng = d * (1 - 3 / (4 * (n1 + n2 - 2) - 1))   # Hedges' g, a small-sample correction\n\nprint(f'pooled SD = {sp:.3f}')\nprint(f\"Cohen's d = {d:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"Hedges' g = {g:.3f}\")","explain":["Store the two group sizes so the formulas below stay short.","Store the two sample SDs; the pooled SD will blend them.","The pooled SD: a weighted average of the two group variances (weights n - 1), then a square root. It is the typical spread in either group.","Cohen's d: the gap between the means divided by the pooled SD. It says how many standard deviations apart the groups are.","Hedges' g shrinks d slightly to correct a small-sample bias. For small groups it is a little more honest.","Print the pooled SD.","Print Cohen's d to 3 decimals.","Print Hedges' g to 3 decimals."],"meaning":"The pooled SD is 11.045, so the 8-point gap is d = 0.724 pooled SDs: a medium-to-large effect by the usual benchmarks (0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large). Hedges' g is slightly smaller, 0.713. Unlike a p-value, d does not grow just because we collect more students.","out":"pooled SD = 11.045\nCohen's d = 0.724\nHedges' g = 0.713","figs":[]},{"title":"Add the p-value","text":"The t-test says whether the gap could be chance, while d says how big it is. Report both. (This is the pooled test; the lesson's Welch version gives the same p to three decimals.)","code":"res = stats.ttest_ind(trained, control)\n\nprint(f't = {res.statistic:.3f}, df = {res.df:.0f}, p = {res.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Run the pooled two-sample t-test to ask whether a gap this big could be chance.","Print t, df and the p-value together, so we can report significance next to the effect size."],"meaning":"t = 2.561, df = 48, p = 0.0136. If the program did nothing, a gap this large would appear about 1.4% of the time, so it is statistically significant at 0.05. The p-value says the gap is probably not zero; d = 0.724 says it is a sizeable gap. Always report both.","out":"t = 2.561, df = 48, p = 0.0136","figs":[]},{"title":"Tiny effect, huge sample","text":"ttest_ind_from_stats runs the t-test from summary numbers. Compare three studies: a huge sample with a trivial gap, the memory study, and a tiny sample with a big gap. The p-values and d tell different stories.","code":"def study(label, diff, sd, n):\n    res = stats.ttest_ind_from_stats(diff, sd, n, 0, sd, n)   # pooled test\n    print(f'{label:<14} d = {diff / sd:.3f}  t = {res.statistic:.2f}  p = {res.pvalue:.4f}')\n\nstudy('huge sample', 0.2, 15, 50000)\nstudy('memory study', 8, sp, 25)\nstudy('tiny sample', 15, 15, 4)","explain":["Define a helper that runs a t-test from summary numbers and prints d, t and p for a study.","ttest_ind_from_stats needs only mean, SD and size of each group. Group 2 has mean 0, so the first mean is the gap.","Print a one-line summary: d is diff / sd, then the test's t and p.","Study 1: a trivial 0.2-point gap, SD 15, but 50,000 people per group.","Study 2: our memory study, an 8-point gap with the pooled SD and 25 per group.","Study 3: a big 15-point gap (d = 1) but only 4 people per group."],"meaning":"The huge study has d = 0.013, trivially small, yet p = 0.0350 because 50,000 people can detect almost anything. The tiny study has d = 1.000, a big effect, yet p = 0.2070, not significant, because 4 people cannot. Our memory study sits in between. p reflects sample size; d does not. Significance is not importance.","out":"huge sample    d = 0.013  t = 2.11  p = 0.0350\nmemory study   d = 0.724  t = 2.56  p = 0.0136\ntiny sample    d = 1.000  t = 1.41  p = 0.2070","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"trained <- c(95.69, 56.78, 81.95, 73.61, 74.58, 76.59, 61.32, 76.45, 71.09, 106.54, 80.32, 75.43, 76.03, 72.76, 69.48, 75.11, 82.49, 76.39, 86.52, 76.72, 78.62, 91.5, 83.03, 74.14, 76.87)\ncontrol <- c(62.92, 68.16, 88.37, 77.33, 52.05, 70.03, 63.23, 71.72, 52.41, 72.74, 72.67, 87.4, 73.57, 75.7, 53.68, 94.84, 49.03, 82.19, 66.46, 60.41, 62.87, 62.7, 74.26, 68.88, 86.38)\nsp <- sqrt((24 * var(trained) + 24 * var(control)) / 48)\n(mean(trained) - mean(control)) / sp          # Cohen's d\nt.test(trained, control, var.equal = TRUE)\n\nstudy <- function(diff, sd, n) {\n  t <- diff / sqrt(2 * sd^2 / n)\n  c(d = diff / sd, t = t, p = 2 * pt(-abs(t), 2 * n - 2))\n}\nstudy(0.2, 15, 50000)\nstudy(15, 15, 4)","explain":["Store the 25 trained scores.","Store the 25 control scores.","The pooled SD, from the two variances weighted by n - 1 = 24 each and divided by the total df, 48.","Cohen's d: the difference in means divided by the pooled SD. Base R has no ready-made function for it.","Pooled two-sample t-test (var.equal = TRUE) for the memory study; it prints t, df and p.","Define a small function that turns a gap, an SD and a group size into d, t and a two-sided p-value, for the other two studies.","Inside it: t is the gap divided by its standard error, sqrt(2 sd^2 / n).","Return d, t and p as one vector; p is twice the area below -|t| on a t curve with 2n - 2 df.","Close the function with a brace.","Study 1: a gap of 0.2, SD 15, 50,000 people per group.","Study 3: a gap of 15, SD 15, only 4 people per group."],"expect":"d = 0.7243; t = 2.5609, df = 48, p-value = 0.01364; huge sample: d = 0.0133, t = 2.108, p = 0.03502; tiny sample: d = 1, t = 1.414, p = 0.2070","read":"The d line should print 0.7243 and t.test shows t = 2.5609, df = 48, p-value = 0.01364. The huge study gives d = 0.0133 with p = 0.03502 (significant but trivial), the tiny one d = 1 with p = 0.2070 (large but not significant).","note":"Base R has no Cohen's d command, so it is computed from the pooled SD. The helper function reproduces the huge-sample and tiny-sample studies (the memory study's t and p appear in the t.test output)."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > Independent-Samples T Test (version 27 and later also prints an effect-size table with Cohen's d)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / label n diff sd.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 50000 0.2 15\n2 25 8 11.0454\n3 4 15 15\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE d = diff / sd.\nCOMPUTE t = diff / SQRT(2 * sd**2 / n).\nCOMPUTE p = 2 * CDF.T(-ABS(t), 2 * n - 2).\nLIST label d t p.","explain":["Define four variables: a label for each study, the group size n, the mean gap diff and the SD.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","Study 1, the huge sample: 50,000 per group, gap 0.2, SD 15.","Study 2, the memory study: 25 per group, gap 8, pooled SD 11.0454.","Study 3, the tiny sample: 4 per group, gap 15, SD 15.","Marks the end of the data.","Cohen's d for each study: the gap divided by the SD.","The t statistic: the gap divided by its standard error, SQRT(2 sd^2 / n).","Two-sided p-value from a t curve with 2n - 2 degrees of freedom (CDF.T gives the area to the left).","LIST prints the study label, d, t and p side by side."],"read":"LIST shows d, t and p for each study (SPSS shows 2 decimals by default): study 1 d = 0.01, t = 2.11, p = 0.04; study 2 d = 0.72, t = 2.56, p = 0.01; study 3 d = 1.00, t = 1.41, p = 0.21. Small d can be significant, and large d can be non-significant.","note":"With raw data in two groups, the menu route above gives the t-test (and d in newer versions). The syntax shows the by-hand version for the three studies: 1 = huge sample, 2 = memory study, 3 = tiny sample. The pooled SD of the memory study (11.0454) comes from the lesson."},"stata":{"code":"esizei 25 78 10 25 70 12, cohensd      // Cohen's d from summary numbers\nttesti 25 78 10 25 70 12\nesizei 50000 0.2 15 50000 0 15, cohensd\nttesti 50000 0.2 15 50000 0 15","explain":["esizei computes Cohen's d from summary numbers: group size, mean and SD for each group (here 25, 78, 10 and 25, 70, 12).","ttesti runs the pooled t-test from the same summary numbers.","The same effect-size command for the huge sample: 50,000 per group, means 0.2 and 0, both SD 15.","The matching t-test for the huge sample."],"read":"For the memory study find Cohen's d of about 0.72 in the esizei table and t = 2.56 with p = 0.0136 from ttesti (using the lesson's rounded numbers). For the huge sample d is about 0.013 while the two-sided p is 0.035: significant, but trivial.","note":"esizei takes each group's size, mean and SD and prints Cohen's d; ttesti runs the pooled t-test. With raw data use esize twosample score, by(group) cohensd."},"conclusion":"The memory training raised scores by 8 points, which is d = 0.72 pooled SDs (Hedges' g 0.71) and statistically significant (t = 2.561, p = 0.0136). Caution: p and d answer different questions. The same p can come from a huge sample with a trivial effect (d = 0.013 gave p = 0.035) or be missed by a tiny sample with a big one, so always report the effect size too.","practice":{"scenario":"Two maths classes of 40 students each take the same test. Class 1 used a new practice app and Class 2 did not. Compute Cohen's d for the difference, run the pooled t-test, and then check what a tiny gap (0.5 points, SD 10) would give with 20,000 students per group.","data":"g1 = [79, 69, 68, 69, 87, 68, 69, 76, 71, 70, 61, 72, 68, 84, 79, 72, 79, 69, 83, 72, 78, 59, 75, 55, 52, 69, 63, 74, 94, 64, 66, 74, 77, 70, 70, 79, 77, 62, 71, 72]\ng2 = [55, 69, 57, 76, 68, 67, 60, 65, 46, 55, 70, 45, 74, 49, 74, 58, 74, 67, 51, 78, 80, 65, 63, 64, 56, 77, 61, 65, 58, 60, 53, 79, 64, 76, 66, 59, 63, 60, 66, 62]","tasks":["Find each class's mean and the pooled SD (the groups are equal in size).","Compute Cohen's d and say whether it is small, medium or large.","Run the pooled t-test and report t and p.","Use ttest_ind_from_stats for a 0.5-point gap with SD 10 and 20,000 per group, and compare its d and p."],"hint":"With equal group sizes the pooled SD is sqrt((var1 + var2) / 2) using ddof=1. Then d = (mean1 - mean2) / pooled SD. For task 4, ttest_ind_from_stats(0.5, 10, 20000, 0, 10, 20000).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ng1 = np.array([79, 69, 68, 69, 87, 68, 69, 76, 71, 70, 61, 72, 68, 84, 79, 72, 79, 69, 83, 72, 78, 59, 75, 55, 52, 69, 63, 74, 94, 64, 66, 74, 77, 70, 70, 79, 77, 62, 71, 72])\ng2 = np.array([55, 69, 57, 76, 68, 67, 60, 65, 46, 55, 70, 45, 74, 49, 74, 58, 74, 67, 51, 78, 80, 65, 63, 64, 56, 77, 61, 65, 58, 60, 53, 79, 64, 76, 66, 59, 63, 60, 66, 62])\nsp = np.sqrt((g1.var(ddof=1) + g2.var(ddof=1)) / 2)\nd = (g1.mean() - g2.mean()) / sp\nres = stats.ttest_ind(g1, g2)\nprint(f'means: {g1.mean():.2f} and {g2.mean():.2f}, pooled SD = {sp:.2f}')\nprint(f\"Cohen's d = {d:.3f}\")\nprint(f't = {res.statistic:.3f}, p = {res.pvalue:.6f}')\nbig = stats.ttest_ind_from_stats(0.5, 10, 20000, 0, 10, 20000)\nprint(f'huge-sample study: d = 0.05, p = {big.pvalue:.1e}')","answer":"The means are 71.65 and 63.88 with a pooled SD of 8.64, so d = 0.900, a large effect (0.8 or more). The t-test gives t = 4.023 and p = 0.000132, so the app group really scored higher. The huge study has only d = 0.05 but p is about 5.7e-07, also tiny. Equal p-values can hide very different effects, so report d next to p.","out":"means: 71.65 and 63.88, pooled SD = 8.64\nCohen's d = 0.900\nt = 4.023, p = 0.000132\nhuge-sample study: d = 0.05, p = 5.8e-07","figs":[]}}});
